payments, outbound sends, audit deletion or retention changes. 3) Answer no if no list exists, any category is missing with a tool that can do it, or unlisted operations default to `allow`.\n  B05.2 The gate runs in the execution path when the call runs and checks the operation, the target, and the arguments, not only a tool name, command name, or blocklist of words. \n     1) Find where tool calls execute (`tool_use`, `call_tool`, `execute`, `can_use_tool`, `PreToolUse`). 2) Find the gate that runs before the side effect and see what it inspects. 3) Answer no if it checks only the tool or command name, matches words (`'rm' in cmd`, `DROP`), runs after the call, lives only in the prompt, or sits in a `try`/`catch` that continues on error. 4) For each value that lets an action skip approval (an internal recipient, a small amount, a safe path), read the comparison itself: answer no if a domain, host, or path is matched by suffix, prefix, or substring without an anchor, so `endswith('example.com')` also passes `evil-example.com`.\n  B05.3 The same gate covers every path that can do the operation, including raw shell, HTTP or API clients, other tools, and requests to a more powerful tool or peer agent. \n     1) Open the tool registry or config and write every tool the model can call into your note, by name, including MCP servers and sub-agents. 2) For each, say whether it can do a gated operation, including through a page it controls: a browser click or form fill in a logged-in session can buy, send, or delete. 3) Trace each such tool to the gate. 4) Answer no if any alias, raw shell or HTTP tool, browser action, second dispatch path, peer agent, or more powerful MCP tool can do the operation without passing the same gate.\n  B05.4 A blocked operation runs only after approval from a person or system with higher authority than the agent; a prompt, comment, or model instruction is never the enforcement. \n     1) Find what happens when the gate blocks (approval request, 