{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SfYatCWWbr7n5dwneWgT8T","type":"tool_result","content":"skill/references/agent.md\nskill/references/build-time.md\nskill/references/configuration.md\nskill/references/run-time.md\nskill/references/agent.md:41:- Revocation delay from cached or self-contained tokens not measured → **B04**\nskill/references/build-time.md:106:- A retrieval search or cache not filtered by the requesting tenant or user → **R04**\nskill/references/build-time.md:108:- Revocation delay from cached or self-contained tokens → **B04**\nskill/references/build-time.md:209:Show that an operator can cut off this agent's access without cutting off the user who launched it. The agent must not need to cooperate. Set and measure the longest time before the cutoff takes effect. Count cached tokens, self-contained tokens, and open sessions. A trusted enforcement point must block later calls within a time limit that fits the potential harm. Revoking only the refresh token is not enough. If tokens live longer than that limit, push the revocation to each service (for example, with OpenID CAEP session-revoked events) or make the tokens shorter-lived.\nskill/references/build-time.md:213:Open a session and revoke the agent identity's access. Then try again, within the set revocation time, through the existing session and a new one. Check self-contained and cached tokens, refresh-token issuing, and open sessions. The agent's requests must fail within the time limit at a trusted enforcement point. The user's unrelated requests must still work. If you push revocations, record when each service received and applied the event.\nskill/references/build-time.md:225:- **B04.3** Later calls are blocked even with cached tokens, self-contained tokens, and open sessions: token lifetime is shorter than the revocation limit, or revocation is pushed to each service (for example CAEP) or checked on every call.\nskill/references/build-time.md:226:  - *How to verify:* 1) Find access-token lifetimes and how services validate tokens (signature only vs `introspect`, deny list, CAEP). 2) Compare lifetime with the stated revocation limit. 3) Answer no if self-contained JWTs outlive the limit and are checked by signature only, cached tokens or open sessions skip the check, or no limit is stated. Answer unknown if validation lives outside this repo.\nskill/references/build-time.md:246:- A gateway or policy point that checks a deny list or introspects tokens on each call, so revocation applies to cached and open sessions.\nskill/references/build-time.md:247:- A documented maximum cutoff time that counts cached tokens, self-contained JWTs, and open sessions.\nskill/references/configuration.md:13:Write a release manifest: a list of everything that makes up this release. Include the container, harness, system prompt, rules files, model, built-in tools, MCP servers, permission scopes, memory and retrieval settings, identity bindings, network rules, and execution budgets. Include security-policy versions, MCP protocol and extension versions, approved tool-publication config, issuer/audience bindings, and cache/task isolation settings. Add a separate model record for every model: the main agent model, sub-agent models, and helper models such as embedding models and rerankers. For each, record the provider, model ID, exact version or checkpoint, and any adapter or fine-tune version. For training or fine-tuning you run yourself, also record the training job ID, base-model version, training code and config version, dataset version, and the digest of the output. The agent app's release number alone is not enough. Use the [model provenance fields](CHECKLIST-VERIFICATION.md#model-provenance-fields) to separate recorded versions from details a provider doesn't share. Check each model ID you request against the provider's versioning docs. Mark it as a pinned snapshot or as an alias that can change, and record which doc you used and its date. Naming rules differ by provider and model generation. Name each artifact's owner and a reference that can't change, where one exists. A machine-readable format, such as CycloneDX ML-BOM or the SPDX 3.0 AI and Dataset profiles, lets tools check the manifest.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ca543b1a-4cf7-4416-b4c0-b3437adb0361","uuid":"1751b16c-39d5-4ac7-b0fd-0423be8bd851","timestamp":"2026-11-21T11:24:16.302Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"skill/references/agent.md\nskill/references/build-time.md\nskill/references/configuration.md\nskill/references/run-time.md\nskill/references/agent.md:41:- Revocation delay from cached or self-contained tokens not measured → **B04**\nskill/references/build-time.md:106:- A retrieval search or cache not filtered by the requesting tenant or user → **R04**\nskill/references/build-time.md:108:- Revocation delay from cached or self-contained tokens → **B04**\nskill/references/build-time.md:209:Show that an operator can cut off this agent's access without cutting off the user who launched it. The agent must not need to cooperate. Set and measure the longest time before the cutoff takes effect. Count cached tokens, self-contained tokens, and open sessions. A trusted enforcement point must block later calls within a time limit that fits the potential harm. Revoking only the refresh token is not enough. If tokens live longer than that limit, push the revocation to each service (for example, with OpenID CAEP session-revoked events) or make the tokens shorter-lived.\nskill/references/build-time.md:213:Open a session and revoke the agent identity's access. Then try again, within the set revocation time, through the existing session and a new one. Check self-contained and cached tokens, refresh-token issuing, and open sessions. The agent's requests must fail within the time limit at a trusted enforcement point. The user's unrelated requests must still work. If you push revocations, record when each service receive