{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EGWdM6WRF1BvMduEgf3NdF","type":"tool_result","content":"7-## B01 · G1 · C4 — Minimal tool surface\n9-**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Security.\n11-### Requirement\n15-### Verification recipe\n50:**Look for:**\n51-- One explicit allowlist of tools per agent role, for example `allowed_tools=[...]` in the Claude Agent SDK, a fixed `tools=[...]` list on an OpenAI Agents SDK `Agent`, or a named list passed to a LangChain/LangGraph agent.\n52-- MCP tools filtered by name against that allowlist after discovery, for example a `tool_filter`, `allowedTools`, or `disallowedTools` setting, or code that drops any tool not on the list.\n53-- A written inventory (README, `.brace/` record, or config comments) that lists every tool, MCP server, API, program, and file path with a task-based reason for each.\n54-- The dispatcher rejects a call to an unknown tool name with an error, instead of looking it up dynamically.\n55-- File access limited to named directories (for example `allowed_directories`, `cwd` sandboxing, or a read-only mount), not the whole filesystem.\n57:**Red flags:**\n58-- Tool list built from `server.list_tools()` or `session.list_tools()` with no allowlist filter, so any tool a server adds becomes callable.\n59-- Allow or auto-approve decisions keyed on server hints such as `readOnlyHint`, `destructiveHint`, or a tool description that says 'read-only'.\n60-- Wildcard tool grants, for example `allowed_tools=['*']`, `mcp__*`, `Bash(*)`, or `permission_mode='bypassPermissions'`.\n61-- A generic tool such as unrestricted shell, `eval`, `exec`, or HTTP fetch to any URL, with no stated task reason.\n62-- `.mcp.json` / `mcp.json` / `claude_desktop_config.json` lists servers that no task in the inventory needs.\n64-**Where to look:**\n65-- Agent construction code (where tools are registered or passed to the model)\n66-- Tool dispatch or router code in the harness\n67-- *.mcp.json, mcp.json, claude_desktop_config.json, settings.json permission blocks\n68-- Claude Agent SDK options (`allowed_tools`, `disallowed_tools`, `permission_mode`, `can_use_tool`)\n69-- Container mounts and volume definitions for file permissions\n116:**Look for:**\n117-- IAM policies that name specific actions and resources, for example Terraform `aws_iam_policy` with listed `Action` and `Resource` ARNs, GCP roles bound to one resource, or Kubernetes `Role` (not `ClusterRole`) with listed verbs.\n118-- Tenant scope enforced in the credential or the data layer, for example a tenant claim in the token, row-level security, or per-tenant service accounts.\n119-- Tokens requested with a set audience (`aud`) or resource indicator, and services that check `aud` on receipt.\n120-- A verified acting-agent identity with an independently recorded delegated subject where applicable, including properly authorized OAuth user-delegation flows.\n121-- Tests that call an allowed read, a forbidden write, an admin action, and another tenant's data with the agent's credentials.\n123:**Red flags:**\n124-- `\"Action\": \"*\"`, `\"Resource\": \"*\"`, `s3:*`, `roles/owner`, `roles/editor`, or `cluster-admin` bound to the agent's identity.\n125-- Agent copies a human login session or uses a personal/admin key without independently verified agent identity and bounded delegation.\n126-- One database login or API key shared across all tenants, with tenant separation done only in the prompt or in app code the model can influence.\n127-- Tokens issued without an audience, or services that accept any valid token from the issuer.\n128-- Kubernetes `automountServiceAccountToken` left on with a broad role the agent doesn't need.\n130-**Where to look:**\n131-- Terraform/CloudFormation/Pulumi IAM files (*.tf, iam*.json)\n132-- Kubernetes Role, ClusterRole, RoleBinding, ServiceAccount manifests\n133-- Token-issuing and auth middleware code (audience and scope settings)\n134-- Database connection setup and row-level security policies\n180:**Look for:**\n181-- Credentials fetched at run time from a managed identity or secrets service, for example AWS IRSA/STS `AssumeRole`, GCP Workload Identity, Azure Managed Identity, Vault dynamic secrets.\n182-- Token lifetimes set in config (for example `DurationSeconds`, `ttl`, `expires_in`) with a stated reason for the value.\n183-- Holder-bound tokens where the service supports them, for example mTLS client certs, DPoP proofs, or workload proof tokens.\n184-- A secret scanner in CI (for example gitleaks, trufflehog, GitHub secret scanning) and a rotation job or policy.\n186:**Red flags:**\n187-- API key read from env and placed in the system prompt or any message the model sees.\n188-- Secrets in `ENV`, `ARG`, or `COPY`'d files in a Dockerfile, or baked into an image layer.\n189-- Long-lived keys checked into config: `.env`, `config.yaml`, `values.yaml`, `terraform.tfvars`, or `.mcp.json` `env` blocks with literal keys.\n190-- Fixed cloud access keys (for example `AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID` in a Kubernetes Secret) when federated or short-lived credentials are available.\n191-- No expiry or rotation set anywhere for a credential the agent uses.\n193-**Where to look:**\n194-- Dockerfile and image build scripts\n195-- System prompt templates and prompt-building code\n196-- .env*, config/*.yaml, Helm values, *.tfvars, *.mcp.json\n197-- Credential/client setup code and CI secret-scan config\n243:**Look for:**\n244-- The agent has its own identity (service account, client ID, workload identity) that can be disabled without touching the launching user's account.\n245-- Short token lifetimes, or a revocation push path such as OpenID CAEP / Shared Signals `session-revoked` events, or token introspection at the enforcement point.\n246-- A gateway or policy point that checks a deny list or introspects tokens on each call, so revocation applies to cached and open sessions.\n247-- A documented maximum cutoff time that counts cached tokens, self-contained JWTs, and open sessions.\n249:**Red flags:**\n250-- The agent acts with the user's own OAuth token, so cutting off the agent means cutting off the user.\n251-- Long-lived self-contained JWTs (hours or days) validated only by signature, with no introspection or revocation check.\n252-- Revocation handled only by revoking the refresh token, while access tokens and open streams stay valid.\n253-- The revoke path calls into the agent process and relies on it to drop its own credentials.\n255-**Where to look:**\n256-- Identity provider and token config (lifetimes, introspection)\n257-- API gateway, service mesh, or auth middleware code\n258-- Ops runbooks or scripts for disabling an agent identity\n260-**Needs runtime evidence:** The measured time from revocation to blocked calls on existing and new sessions, while the user's own requests still work. Recipe B04 is a timed drill; code cannot show it.\n307:**Look for:**\n308-- A gate in the execution path (not the prompt) that blocks named high-impact operations by default: deletes, destructive DB updates, force-push, infra teardown, payments, outbound sends, and audit-log deletion or retention changes.\n309-- The gate checks operation, target, and arguments against policy at the moment of execution, for example a `can_use_tool` callback or `PreToolUse` hook in the Claude Agent SDK, `needs_approval` / tool guardrails in the OpenAI Agents SDK, or LangGraph `interrupt()` before a tool node.\n310-- Approval required from a reviewer or system with higher authority than the agent, for each specific action.\n311-- Raw paths covered: shell, SQL, and generic HTTP tools route through the same gate, or are removed.\n312-- A written record of which of the three capabilities each agent type has (untrusted input, sensitive access, outside action), and either all consequential actions gated or a design such as plan-then-execute, a two-model pattern, or CaMeL-style capability tracking.\n314:**Red flags:**\n315:- A blocklist of command names or words (for example `if 'rm' in cmd` or `DENY=['DROP','delete']`) treated as the control; this is a red flag, not a pass.\n316-- Approval asked of the model itself, or enforced only by a system-prompt rule like 'never delete without asking'.\n317-- A generic shell or SQL tool that can do the same delete or send as a gated tool but skips the gate.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ca543b1a-4cf7-4416-b4c0-b3437adb0361","uuid":"bde27273-e52d-4d45-9831-7638f3f9981a","timestamp":"2026-11-21T11:24:21.196Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"7-## B01 · G1 · C4 — Minimal tool surface\n9-**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Security.\n11-### Requirement\n15-### Verification recipe\n50:**Look for:**\n51-- One explicit allowlist of tools per agent role, for example `allowed_tools=[...]` in the Claude Agent SDK, a fixed `tools=[...]` list on an OpenAI Agents SDK `Agent`, or a named list passed to a LangChain/LangGraph agent.\n52-- MCP tools filtered by name against that allowlist after discovery, for example a `tool_filter`, `allowedTools`, or `disallowedTools` setting, or code that drops any tool not on the list.\n53-- A written inventory (README, `.brace/` record, or config comments) that lists every tool, MCP server, API, program, and file path with a task-based reason for each.\n54-- The dispatcher rejects a call to an unknown tool name with an error, instead of looking it up dynamically.\n55-- File access limited to named directories (for example `allowed_directories`, `cwd` sandboxing, or a read-only mount), not the whole filesystem.\n57:**Red flags:**\n58-- Tool list built from `server.list_tools()` or `session.list_tools()` with no allowlist filter, so any tool a server adds becomes callable.\n59-- Allow or auto-approve decisions keyed on server hints such as `readOnlyHint`, `destructiveHint`, or a tool description that says 'read-only'.\n60-- Wildcard tool grants, for example `allowed_tools=['*']`, `mcp__*`, `Bash(*)`, or `permission_mode='bypassPermissions'`.\n61-- A generic tool such as unrestricted shell, `eval`, `exec`, or HTTP fetch to any URL, with no stated task reason.\n62-- `.mcp.json` / `mcp.json` / `claude_desktop_config.json` lists servers that no task in the inventory needs.\n64-**Where to look:**\n65-- Agent construction code (where tools are registered or passed to the model)\n66-- Tool dispatch or router code in the harness\n67-- *.mcp.json, mcp.json, claude_desktop_config.json, settings.json permission blocks\n68-- Claude Agent SDK options (`allowed_tools`, `disallowed_tools`, `permission_mode`, `can_use_tool`)\n69-- Container mounts and volume definitions for file permissions\n116:**Look for:**\n117-- IAM policies that name specific actions and resources, for example Terraform `aws_iam_policy` with listed `Action` and `Resource` ARNs, GCP roles bound to one resource, or Kubernetes `Role` (not `ClusterRole`) with listed verbs.\n118-- Tenant scope enforced in the credential or the data layer, for example a tenant claim in the token, row-level security, or per-tenant service accounts.\n119-- Tokens requested with a set audience (`aud`) or resource indicator, and services that check `aud` on receipt.\n120-- A verified acting-agent identity with an independently recorded delegated subject where applicable, including properly authorized OAuth user-delegation flows.\n121-- Tests that call an allowed read, a forbidden write, an admin action, and another tenant's data with the agent's credentials.\n123:**Red flags:**\n124-- `\"Action\": \"*\"`, `\"Resource\": \"*\"`, `s3:*`, `roles/owner`, `roles/editor`, or `cluster-admin` bound to the agent's identity.\n125-- Agent copies a human login session or uses a personal/admin key without independently verified agent identity and bounded delegation.\n126-- One database login or API key shared across all tenants, with tenant separation done only in the prompt or in app code the model can influence.\n127-- Tokens issued without an audience, or services that accept any valid token from the issuer.\n128-- Kubernetes `automountServiceAccountToken` left on with a broad role the agent doesn't need.\n130-**Where to look:**\n131-- Terraform/CloudFormation/Pulumi IAM files (*.tf, iam*.json)\n132-- Kubernetes Role, ClusterRole, RoleBinding, ServiceAccount manifests\n133-- Token-issuing and auth middleware code (audience and scope settings)\n134-- Database connection setup and row-level security policies\n180:**Look for:**\n181-- Credentials fetched at run time from a managed identity or secrets service, for example AWS IRSA/STS `AssumeRole`, GCP Workload Identity, Azure Managed Identity, Vault dynamic secrets.\n182-- Token lifetimes set in config (for example `DurationSeconds`, `ttl`, `expires_in`) with a stated reason for the value.\n183-- Holder-bound tokens where the service supports them, for example mTLS client certs, DPoP proofs, or workload proof tokens.\n184-- A secret scanner in CI (for example gitleaks, trufflehog, GitHub secret scanning) and a rotation job or policy.\n186:**Red flags:**\n187-- API key read from env and placed in the system prompt or any message the model sees.\n188-- Secrets in `ENV`, `ARG`, or `COPY`'d files in a Dockerfile, or baked into an image layer.\n189-- Long-lived keys checked into config: `.env`, `config.yaml`, `values.yaml`, `terraform.tfvars`, or `.mcp.json` `env` blocks with literal keys.\n190-- Fixed cloud access keys (for example `AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID` in a Kubernetes Secret) when federated or short-lived credentials are available.\n191-- No expiry or rotation set anywhere for a credential the agent uses.\n193-**Where to look:**\n194-- Dockerfile and image build scripts\n195-- System prompt templates and prompt-building code\n196-- .env*, config/*.yaml, Helm values, *.tfvars, *.mcp.json\n197-- Credential/client setup code and CI secret-scan config\n243:**Look for:**\n244-- The agent has its own identity (service account, client ID, workload identity) that can be disabled without touching the launching user's account.\n245-- Short token lifetimes, or a revocation push path such as OpenID CAEP / Shared Signals `session-revoked` events, or token introspection at the enforcement point.\n246-- A gateway or policy point that checks a deny list or introspects tokens on each call, so revocation applies to cached and open sessions.\n247-- A documented maximum cutoff time that counts cached tokens, self-contained JWTs, and open sessions.\n249:**Red flags:**\n250-- The agent acts with the user's own OAuth token, so cutting off the agent means cutting off the user.\n251-- Long-lived self-contained JWTs (hours or days) validated only by signature, with no introspection or revocation check.\n252-- Revocation handled only by revoking the refresh token, while access tokens and open streams stay valid.\n253-- The revoke path calls into the agent process and relies on it to drop its own credentials.\n255-**Where to look:**\n256-- Identity provider and token config (lifetimes, introspection)\n257-- API gateway, service mesh, or auth middleware code\n258-- Ops runbooks or scripts for disabling an agent identity\n260-**Needs runtime evidence:** The measured time from revocation to blocked calls on existing and new sessions, while the user's own requests still work. Recipe B04 is a timed drill; code cannot show it.\n307:**Look for:**\n308-- A gate in the execution path (not the prompt) that blocks named high-impact operations by default: deletes, destructive DB updates, force-push, infra teardown, payments, outbound sends, and audit-log deletion or retention changes.\n309-- The gate checks operation, target, and arguments against policy at the moment of execution, for example a `can_use_tool` callback or `PreToolUse` hook in the Claude Agent SDK, `needs_approval` / tool guardrails in the OpenAI Agents SDK, or LangGraph `interrupt()` before a tool node.\n310-- Approval required from a reviewer or system with higher authority than the agent, for each specific action.\n311-- Raw paths covered: shell, SQL, and generic HTTP tools route through the same gate, or are removed.\n312-- A written record of which of the three capabilities each agent type has (untrusted input, sensitive access, outside action), and either all consequential actions gated or a design such as plan-then-execute, a two-model pattern, or CaMeL-style capability tracking.\n314:**Red flags:**\n315:- A blocklist of command names or words (for example `if 'rm' in cmd` or `DENY=['DROP','delete']`) treated as the control; this is a red flag, not a pass.\n316-- Approval asked of the model itself, or enforced only by a system-prompt rule like 'never delete without asking'.\n317-- A generic shell or SQL tool that can do the same delete or send as a gated tool but skips the gate.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01EGWdM6WRF1BvMduEgf3NdF","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
